#let p = problem.with(code: "MM7033", unit: "U1")
#let items = (
 p(1,"E","U1.1 Minimal polynomials",[Find the minimal polynomial and degree of $sqrt(11)$ over $QQ$.],[Use a monic quadratic.],[Eisenstein applies at eleven.],[$x^2-11$ is monic, irreducible by Eisenstein, and vanishes at the element. It is the minimal polynomial, so the degree is two.],[Verify all three properties.]),
 p(2,"E","U1.1 Splitting fields",[Find a splitting field of $x^2-3$ over $QQ$.],[Both roots differ by a sign.],[Adjoining either root supplies the other.],[The field $QQ(sqrt(3))$ contains both roots $±sqrt(3)$ and is generated by them, so it is a splitting field. It has degree two because the polynomial is irreducible.],[State the generation condition as well as splitting.]),
 p(3,"E","U1.2 Finite-field subfields",[List the subfield sizes in $FF_(3^4)$.],[Use divisors of four.],[The degrees are one, two, and four.],[The subfields have sizes $3,9,81$, each unique. No other degree can occur by the tower law, and the finite-field subfield theorem supplies these three.],[Include uniqueness and exhaustiveness.]),
 p(4,"E","U1.2 Separability",[Show $x^5-x$ over $FF_5$ has no repeated roots.],[Compute the formal derivative in characteristic five.],[It is negative one.],[The derivative is $5x^4-1=-1$, so its gcd with the polynomial is one. Thus the polynomial is separable. It in fact has all five field elements as roots.],[Use the derivative criterion, with the correct characteristic.]),
 p(5,"C","U1.1 Splitting-field degree",[Find the degree of the splitting field of $x^3-3$ over $QQ$.],[Adjoin a real cube root and a primitive cube root of unity.],[The first degree is three, and the second is two because the first field is real.],[Eisenstein gives $[QQ(alpha):QQ]=3$ for the real root $alpha$. The other roots are $alpha zeta,alpha zeta^2$, so the splitting field is $QQ(alpha,zeta)$. Since $QQ(alpha)$ is real, it does not contain nonreal $zeta$, whose quadratic polynomial is $x^2+x+1$. The total degree is six.],[Justify both steps of the tower.]),
 p(6,"C","U1.1 Embeddings",[Describe all $QQ$-embeddings of $QQ(sqrt(2))$ into $CC$.],[The image of the generator must satisfy its minimal polynomial.],[There are two choices of root.],[The generator can map only to $sqrt(2)$ or $-sqrt(2)$. Each choice extends uniquely to an embedding by the root-extension theorem, with formula $a+b sqrt(2)|->a±b sqrt(2)$. Hence there are exactly two.],[Show the possible images actually define embeddings.]),
 p(7,"C","U1.2 Frobenius order",[Prove Frobenius on $FF_(p^n)$ has order exactly $n$.],[Its $n$th power is the identity.],[A smaller positive power would make all elements roots of a polynomial of degree less than $p^n$.],[The $n$th iterate is $a|->a^(p^n)=a$. If the $k$th iterate were identity for $0<k<n$, all $p^n$ elements would solve $x^(p^k)-x=0$, contradicting the root bound since $p^k<p^n$. Thus the order is exactly $n$.],[Exclude every smaller positive exponent.]),
 p(8,"C","U1.2 Derivatives",[Prove a polynomial over a field of characteristic $p$ has derivative zero exactly when all nonzero terms have exponents divisible by $p$.],[Different monomials in the derivative cannot cancel each other's coefficients.],[The coefficient of $x^(i-1)$ is $i a_i$.],[The derivative is zero precisely when each $i a_i=0$. If $a_i!=0$, the field property makes this equivalent to $i=0$ in the prime field, meaning $p|i$. The converse follows by differentiating such monomials.],[Use uniqueness of polynomial coefficients.]),
 p(9,"C","U1.1 Changing the base",[Compare the minimal polynomial of $sqrt(2)$ over $QQ$ and over $QQ(sqrt(2),sqrt(3))$.],[The element already belongs to the larger base.],[Its degree over that base is one.],[Over $QQ$ the polynomial is $x^2-2$. Over the larger field it is $x-sqrt(2)$. Minimal polynomials and degrees depend on the specified base, even when the element is unchanged.],[State both coefficient fields explicitly.]),
 p(10,"A","U1.1 Quartic splitting field",[Prove the splitting field of $x^4-2$ over $QQ$ has degree eight.],[Let $alpha$ be the positive real fourth root of two.],[Its roots are $±alpha,±i alpha$.],[Let $alpha$ be the positive real fourth root of two. Eisenstein at two gives degree four for $QQ(alpha) slash QQ$. The splitting field is $QQ(alpha,i)$, since adjoining $i$ supplies all four roots and the ratio of $i alpha$ to $alpha$ recovers $i$. The field $QQ(alpha)$ is real, so $x^2+1$ has no root there and adjoining $i$ has degree two. The tower degree is eight.],[Prove the named field is generated by the roots and compute the relative quadratic degree.]),
 p(11,"A","U1.2 Subfield intersections",[Inside $FF_(p^12)$, determine the intersection of its subfields of orders $p^4$ and $p^6$.],[The intersection degree divides both four and six.],[Both subfields contain the unique field of order $p^2$.],[The intersection is a subfield, so its degree over $FF_p$ divides four and six, hence divides two. Both larger subfields contain the unique order-$p^2$ subfield, because two divides both degrees and uniqueness identifies their copies inside the ambient field. Thus the intersection has degree at least two and therefore exactly two.],[Prove both a lower and upper bound on the intersection.]),
 p(12,
  "A",
  "U1.2 Inseparable irreducibles",
  [Prove $x^p-t$ is irreducible and inseparable over $FF_p(t)$.],
  [View it first over the UFD $FF_p[t]$.],
  [Use Eisenstein at the prime element $t$, then the derivative.],
  [*Finding the approach.* Treat the polynomial first over the UFD $FF_p[t]$, where the prime element $t$ is available. The passage to its fraction field is a separate step.

*Argument.* It is primitive and Eisenstein at $t$: all nonleading coefficients are divisible by $t$ and the constant term is not divisible by $t^2$. Gauss's lemma gives irreducibility over the fraction field. Its derivative in $x$ is zero. In a splitting field with root $alpha$, the characteristic-$p$ identity gives $x^p-t=(x-alpha)^p$, so its root repeats and it is inseparable.

The leading coefficient is one, so the polynomial is primitive. The zero intermediate coefficients are divisible by $t$, and the constant $-t$ is divisible by $t$ but not $t^2$. Eisenstein therefore applies in the polynomial variable $x$. In characteristic $p$, every intermediate binomial coefficient in $(x-alpha)^p$ vanishes. Since $alpha^p=t$, this expansion shows one root with multiplicity $p$, not merely that a formal derivative happens to be zero.

*A tempting dead end.* A zero derivative does not by itself prove irreducibility. Conversely, an irreducible polynomial over a nonperfect field need not have distinct roots.

*Hypotheses.* The characteristic is the prime $p$, and $t$ is an indeterminate, not a chosen element of a finite field. The coefficient field $FF_p(t)$ need not be perfect.],
  [Explain why the finite-field separability theorem does not apply to the infinite coefficient field.],
  idea: [Use the prime t to prove irreducibility, then the characteristic-p binomial identity to exhibit inseparability.]),
 p(13,
  "A",
  "U1 Transfer: Degrees inside a finite field",
  [How many elements of $FF_64$ have minimal polynomial of degree six over $FF_2$? Justify that every smaller possible degree has been accounted for.],
  [An element generates a subfield, and its degree divides six.],
  [The proper subfields have four and eight elements and intersect in the two-element field.],
  [*Finding the approach.* Count the elements lying in proper subfields, taking care not to count their intersection twice. Field degree restricts which subfields are possible.

*Argument.* For $alpha in FF_64$, the tower law shows $[FF_2(alpha):FF_2]$ divides six, so it is one, two, three, or six. Finite-field subfield structure gives unique subfields of orders two, four, and eight. The two-element field is contained in both larger proper subfields. Their intersection has degree dividing both two and three, hence degree one and size two. Thus exactly $4+8-2=10$ elements have degree less than six. The remaining $64-10=54$ have degree six. Equivalently these form nine Frobenius orbits of size six, corresponding to nine monic irreducible degree-six polynomials.

*A tempting dead end.* Subtracting the sizes two, four, and eight counts the two-element field repeatedly. Also excluding only the prime field misses elements of degrees two and three.

*Hypotheses.* The count uses the uniqueness of each finite subfield and the tower divisibility for intermediate fields. It includes zero among the smaller-degree elements.],
  [List all degree divisors, identify the intersection, and justify the subtraction.],
  optional: true,
  minutes: 60,
  idea: [Replace a minimal-polynomial degree condition by membership in the union of proper subfields.]),
)
